The most accurate initial measurements of Pluto's diameter came from brightness measurements made during mutual eclipses of Pluto and Charon. The subtle shifts in reflected light as they passed in front of each other provided incredibly precise data about their relative sizes and distances, allowing astronomers to calculate Pluto's diameter with a remarkable degree of accuracy back in the late 1980s. It was a truly ingenious method!
